Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:18.790 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  whether you're a longtime zelda fan or a  newcomer to the series chances are  you're at least familiar with the lost  woods it's a recurring location dating  back all the way to the very first title  and has always had a sense of mystery  attached to it its reputation is mainly  that of a dense forbidden forest that is  hard to navigate causing some to get  lost hence the name there have been  quite a lot of iterations of the lost  woods each with their own rules  atmosphere and end goal sometimes it's  the resting place of the master sword  sometimes it houses a dungeon or both it  differs for each game now in most games  the forest is simply very confusing and  those who are unlucky or ill-prepared  run the risk of becoming irreversibly  lost forever never to be seen again but  there's one version of the lost woods  that stands out from all the others that  being the one from ocarina of time the  reason it stands out is because here the  punishment for getting lost is a lot  more severe and honestly quite  disturbing getting lost and potentially  dying of exhaustion dehydration or  starvation is already bad enough but in  ocarina of time legends say that those  who find themselves lost will be cursed  to wander the forest for all eternity  but is any of this true are the souls of  the unfortunate really wandering the  forest or are these simply old folk  tales perhaps to deter people from  setting foot inside a way to protect the  forest and the secrets that lie within  as far as the claims about the lost  woods go there's only one example of  something close to a first-hand witness  account that potentially proves the  validity of these stories an individual  who's been bold enough or reckless  enough to venture inside the forbidden  parts of the forest never to be seen  again this will be a two-part theory  about the lost woods in ocarina of time  and to a smaller extent majora's mask in  today's video we'll be looking at the  woods themselves and the stories  surrounding them and in part 2 which  will be released soon after we will  attempt to shed some light on the  missing person's case and the mysterious  circumstances surrounding his  disappearance based on today's findings  so sit back relax and let's dive into  the curse of the lost woods  [Music]  the stories surrounding the lost woods  are not as straightforward as it may  seem there is a degree of uncertainty  misunderstandings and even contradictory  information about the rules the sacred  forest abides to especially when it  comes to some of the consequences and  punishment the forest as a whole is made  up of three primary locations the kokiri  forest where the children of the forest  live the lost woods themselves and the  sacred forest meadow which also houses  the ever so mysterious forest temple  although separate areas each of these  locations ultimately makes up the forest  as a whole so when people in the game  talk about the forest they can be  referring to any of these locations the  kokiri forest is as stated before the  home of the forest children known as the  one thing to note right off the bat is  that yes the kokiri are the same  entities as the koroks who show up for  the first time in the wind waker here  it's explained that after the events of  ocarina of time the kokiri took on the  form of the koroks following the great  flood we even meet the ghost of a sage  who is confirmed to be the ancestor of  the koroks who still takes on the form  of a kokiri child akin to the ones from  ocarina of time one rule states that the  children of the forest can never leave  the area as they will die if they do but  there's been a lot of debate about how  this even works and if it's even true to  begin with the biggest reason why people  have put this into question is because  during the credits of ocarina of time  the kokiri are seen celebrating  alongside the other inhabitants of  hyrule and this celebration takes place  on long long range far outside the  forest now there could be many reasons  why this is possible nobody ever makes  it clear how and why a kokiri would die  if they step outside the forest the  kokiri themselves take it very seriously  and seem to think that it spells instant  death for them if they try but again we  never see the effect it has on them some  suggest that they simply lose their  immortality if they leave and grow old  and die others think that because the  kokiri are vulnerable children the  dangers of the outside world is what  gets them yet others claim that this  entire story is simply a lie made up by  the deku tree to discourage them from  leaving i myself think it's a  combination of the last two the story  may have indeed been made up it wouldn't  be the first time that the deku tree  used a white lie to keep secrets case  and point being link's true origin as a  hylian which he didn't disclose until  after link was already an adult and  never told any of the other kokiri about  it this was of course to link's own  benefit he sensed great destiny in him  and wanted to keep him safe until he was  ready to fulfill said destiny the deku  tree's purpose is protecting and  watching over the forest and the spirits  that dwell within and it's likely that a  kokiri out on his own would run the risk  of death if faced with monsters or other  threats from the outside world i however  don't think that there's any magic  involved where a kokiri would magically  drop dead upon leaving the forest or  lose their immortality and die a slow  death and there's a couple of reasons  why i think this is not the case the  first is that there's no evidence of a  kokiri who lost our immortality in any  of the games we never meet a former  kokiri who has chosen a mortal life  outside the forest second is the fact  that in every other game the spirits of  the forest are able to leave just fine  in fact in the case of the wind waker  the deku tree even encourages them to  leave to  well spread his seed so the forest can  expand and of course in breath of the  wild they're all over the place lastly  there's a gossip stone right outside  link's house which says they say that  one kokiri has left the forest but he's  still alive now of course this could be  referring to link himself but there are  some problems with that idea the gossip  stone is already there before link  leaves the forest first of all and  second link isn't a kokiri he's a hylian  so could it be that this gossip stone  isn't referring to link and is instead  talking about a real kokiri who secretly  left the forest one day and has lived to  tell the tale maybe but as to who this  mysterious kokiri would be is anyone's  guess so whether true or not this is the  rule set to apply to those who call the  forest home but what about the other way  around what happens to outsiders trying  to enter well this is where we get to  the most notorious part about the lost  woods in ocarina of time it is said that  those from the outside world who enter  the forest and end up getting lost will  transform into a monster and find  themselves trapped here forever  additionally their age will dictate what  shape they will take on lost children  are set to transform into a skull kid  little forest imps made of wood and if  it's an adult they will take on the form  of a stealthos the wandering remains of  a skeleton turn to a hostile monster  again we have to ask ourselves is any of  this true do outsiders really transform  into cursed monstrosities or is it  potentially yet another lie or fairy  tale this time to keep people out as  opposed to keeping them in let's start  with the skull kids one theory i  disagree with is the idea that kokiri  who get lost in the woods turn into  skull kids i've read about this idea in  a couple of different places most  notably on zelda wiki which says that  quote there's speculation about whether  or not kokiri or hylian children turn  into skull kids it is more plausible  that kokiri turn into skull kids due to  the fact that they stay children for  their whole lives and it further states  that quote skull kids appear to be made  of wood and later games make it clear  that kokiri are related to the tree like  koroks who also hide their faces with  masks and yes there are some  similarities between the koroks and the  skull kids but for me personally it's  not enough to come to this conclusion  i'd even argue that there's more  evidence that contradicts it we know for  a fact that the skull kids and the  kokiri have a friendly relationship with  each other they are said to play with  them and zarya has even taught her  famous song to one of them this is also  confirmed to be the very same skull kid  who later plays a big role in majora's  mask at the end of the game he remarks  that link has the same smell as the  fairy kid who taught him a song in the  woods that fairy kid of course being  saria who did indeed teach him the song  and was allegedly even friends with him  as mido points out that she only taught  the song to close friends so as far as  counter evidence goes if the lost woods  are so dangerous that they could make a  kokiri who accidentally makes a wrong  turn transform into a skull kid why  would the deku tree allow them to go  inside in the first place because that's  what they do zarya has our own secret  spot in the forest meadow and both mido  and fedo are seen visiting the lost  woods as well on top of that one of them  even says to link if you're looking for  sarya she's in the lost woods as usual i  know you'll get lost but don't worry  you'll just end up back at the entrance  i doubt that if the kokiri ran any risk  of being cursed and turned into a skull  kid they would be so casual about it  some have used this line from one of the  kokiri as evidence that one of the quote  unquote forest kids was complaining  about their face some seem to think that  they are referring to a kokiri who was  complaining about their face and because  there's a skull kid who does something  similar that skull kid must have been a  former kokiri that they knew but if you  ask me it's pretty obvious that they are  simply referring directly to the skull  kid and not one of their kokiri friends  the term forest kid is probably just a  name that the kokiri used to describe  them it's clearly meant to be a hint as  to who to sell the skull mask to in  kakariko village we find a similar hint  about the kid from the graveyard lastly  there is a gossip stone that sheds some  more light on the story about the lost  woods stating that they say that when  non-fairy folk enter the lost woods they  become monsters the term non-fairy folk  is of course already pretty damning for  the kokiri skull kit theory but it's  also important to note that skull kids  are not monsters they are spirits  something we'll get back to later for  now let's move on to adults and their  transformation into stealthos  the lore behind this transformation  stems from a conversation with one of  the kokiri girls inside the lost woods  there's two versions of this  conversation one from the original and  one from the 3ds title which is slightly  different on the n64 she says anybody  who comes into the forest will be lost  everybody will become a stealthos  everybody stealthos but on the 3ds she  says except for the kokiri people who  come into the forest end up lost they  all become stealthos notice that on the  3ds they added the phrase except for the  kokiri another reason to believe that  the kokiri are not the ones who get lost  and turn into skull kids this  conversation also ties directly into our  missing person's case which we'll cover  next time but let's just say for now  that his disappearance stands as the  most compelling piece of evidence that  at least parts of the legends about the  lost woods may in fact be true and that  he has indeed been lost to the forest  cursed to live his life as a skeletal  monstrosity for who knows how long maybe  until link or someone else puts him out  of his misery but there's a couple of  things that never sat right with me  regarding this case and maybe there's a  lot more to it than we are led to  believe it doesn't have to be of course  maybe the simplest answer is also the  right one but this wouldn't be much of a  theory video if we didn't at least try  to come up with an alternative the first  and most obvious question people have  asked is of course why do the lost woods  not affect link i mean he is a hylian  after all but to be fair i don't think  this necessarily takes away from the  possibility that the lost woods are  indeed cursed because the gossip stone  we mentioned earlier does clearly say  that non-fairy folk are said to turn  into monsters and link does have a fairy  it's easy to believe that guardian  fairies can somehow protect their  companion against the curse what it  doesn't explain however is how hylians  and perhaps other races were able to  build all these structures inside the  forest there's man-made entrances to  zora's domain and goron city as well as  of course the huge forest temple if the  woods are cursed how did they manage to  build all of this and not get affected  by it now you might say well maybe the  curse didn't go into effect until after  the forest temple fell but that doesn't  seem to add up either after all some of  the skull kids particularly the one from  majira's mask have been around since  ancient times this means that if the  stories about children turning into  skull kids are true the curse has to  have been around for a very very long  time now the forest temple is very much  haunted by ghosts and monsters including  stealthos but the general consensus is  that this is because a battle took place  here and the temple was at some point  invaded leading to the demise of its  occupants who then continued to linger  inside their resurrection may have even  been solely ganondorf's doing as sarya  does state that the temple has only  recently become infested by monsters the  stories surrounding the forest temple  are too much to cover in this video but  if you want to check out some cool  theories about it i will add one by  vortexi gaming as well as one from  celtic in the description below either  way it's clear that there has been  highly inactivity in the lost woods  possibly as late as the hyrulean civil  war which ended only a few years before  link started his adventure while the  presence of stalfos inside the forest  temple does seem to hint that the curse  is real this isn't the only option if  adults who enter the forest indeed turn  into stealthos how come we only find  them inside the temple and nowhere else  in the forest why are they also found  inside the shadow temple and ganon's  castle among other places before we can  try to answer these questions we first  have to determine what a stalfose even  is in the first place the game itself  doesn't have much to say about them  other than how to defeat it but i did  find two passages in hyrule historia and  encyclopedia two books which seem to be  a bit at odds with each other when it  comes to the lore behind the stalfos  historia simply repeats what's already  been established by the game itself that  adults turn into stealthos and children  into skull kids and that both are doomed  to wander the woods however encyclopedia  adds to the stealthos lore and suggests  that they are specifically the  resurrected remains of soldiers who fell  in battle they have retained parts of  their memory in particular their combat  skills now normally i'm not one to  choose a book over a game but since the  game doesn't have much to say about the  stealthos for the theory at hand i think  it's fair to at least take the books  into consideration while they do without  a doubt hold information that is  questionable possible retcons or  flat-out mistakes at the same time  there's also plenty of stuff that has  been accepted as canon like you know the  official zelda timeline or the fact that  the hero shade is the hero of time which  was just a theory until the book  confirmed it obviously i'm biased  because i'm trying to build a theory so  take it with a grain of salt but for now  let's go with the notion that stalfos  are indeed dead soldiers to me this  simply makes the most sense given their  weapons and combat skills this makes the  appearance of stealth in places like  gannon's castle the shadow temple and  forest temple make perfect sense these  would have been locations where soldiers  were to be stationed a wartime prison  for the enemies of the royal family the  remains of hyrule castle which  previously housed plenty of soldiers and  a mysterious stronghold inside a forest  where a battle took place so then why  would our missing person turn into a  stealthos he's not a soldier we know  that you'd expect him to turn into a  re-dead or something in fact he already  looks a bit like a redead we also never  actually witness him as a stealth foes  in the game even after clearing the  forest temple first nor do we see other  stealthos outside the forest temple so  could it be that the legends behind the  stealthos and maybe even the skull kit  transformations are in fact just that  legends we've already demonstrated that  the stories about the kokiri dying when  they leave the forest could have been a  lie this opens up the possibility that  the same thing applies to the mythos  surrounding the lost woods at the very  least it could have been a  misinterpretation people undoubtedly  went missing in the forest and were  never seen again maybe people witnessed  skull kids and the stalfos in and around  the forest temple and simply drew the  conclusion that these must be the  remains of these missing people while in  fact the two could be unrelated similar  to those who are said to be the spirits  of the undead unable to move on to the  afterlife because of hatred or  unfulfilled desires maybe stealthos are  indeed the remains of dead soldiers who  are also unable to find peace after they  perished in battle as for the skull kids  they could simply be just another type  of forest spirit just like the kokiri  and may have no connection to hylian  children at all spirits come in all  sorts of different forms in the zelda  universe from dragons to great fairies  little woodland imps to other mysterious  creatures not saying that skull kids  cannot possibly have been hylian  children in their past lives i'm just  saying that it could also be a myth no  skull kid has ever shed light on what  they are who they were in a past life or  anything else about their kind they are  to this day a bit of an enigma so it's  not hard to believe that the hylians may  have misunderstood them and made up  stories about their origin due to a lack  of knowledge the use of words like it is  said and they say does seem to reflect  that these are stories being passed down  and not necessarily solid facts it is of  course also noteworthy that this is the  only game where the lost woods act this  way in any other game it's just a  mysterious place where people can  potentially get lost there's no mentions  of transforming into the undead or  becoming spirits or anything else like  it in a link to the past some people  like thieves even live and hide inside  the forest to avoid persecution i do  realize that not all iterations of the  lost woods are the same place but it is  a bit strange that this legendary curse  only exists here and nowhere else so are  the lost woods cursed we may never know  for sure the fact that people have been  speculating and debating these mysteries  for so many years is a testament to the  fact that the game doesn't spell it out  clear as day although the disappearance  of one of the game's npcs is definitely  suspicious like i said we never actually  witnessed them turning into a stalfos  all we have is hearsay and as we all  know not every story told ends up being  true so in the next video we'll take a  closer look at our missing persons case  we'll look at their background and the  circumstances and do a little sherlock  holmes detective work to see if there's  more to this story than we initially  thought until then i hope to see you in  the next video this is dawn signing off  and have a good one  [Music]  do  [Music]  you

